{"name":"Blue Rinse","hex":"#b7bdc6","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue rinse","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A diluted blue hair dye used to reduce the yellowed or translucent appearance of greying hair on older women (because the ability to see the color blue decreases with age)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_rinse"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue rinse","blocks":[{"text":"A blue rinse is a dilute hair dye used to reduce the yellowed appearance of grey or white hair."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Women_with_blue_rinses.jpg/330px-Women_with_blue_rinses.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_rinse","wikidata":"Q4930150"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/2010._%D0%94%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%86%D0%BA._%D0%9A%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%8C_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0_010.jpg/500px-2010._%D0%94%D0%BE%D